WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.697
The first step in text cleaning is inspection.

00:00:02.697 --> 00:00:06.702
Run this query and look closely at names, emails, and addresses.

00:00:06.702 --> 00:00:11.042
Do not decide on a cleaning rule before you understand the patterns that actually occur.

00:00:11.042 --> 00:00:16.047
Some values may have extra spaces, inconsistent capitalization, or abbreviations.

00:00:16.047 --> 00:00:21.442
The cleaning rule should respond to observed problems, not blindly transform every string.

00:00:21.442 --> 00:00:25.897
Connect this idea to a CRM standardizing names, emails, and phone numbers.

00:00:25.897 --> 00:00:32.567
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:37.692
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:37.692 --> 00:00:42.882
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:42.882 --> 00:00:48.240
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
